Altitude review of the phase 1a-1d diff (RC2/RC3/RC4/RC6) against DR-2026-09-26. Findings as JSON list of {file, line, summary, deeper_fix}:

```json
[
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 4272,
    "summary": "close_adversarial_pass() re-implements 'resolve a path safely under the repo root' from scratch (a raw '..' string check on Path.parts, then .resolve() + REPO_ROOT-in-parents test), a weaker, independent duplicate of the hardened _resolve_transcript_path() helper defined 300 lines earlier in the same file (line 3943) specifically because a path-traversal bypass of that first check was found and fixed by an adversarial review.",
    "deeper_fix": "Generalize the fix at the same depth it was actually found: add one dbcore.py helper, e.g. dbcore.resolve_under(root: Path, rel_path: str) -> Path | None, that does the normpath + resolve() + relative_to() containment check once, and have both the transcript refusal and the SURVIVED-artefact refusal call it. As written, the hardening from the adversarial review is a one-off patch on a single call site in one writer; the next writer that validates an on-disk artefact path (RC1/phase 2's search_execution_artefacts refusals need exactly this) will have to rediscover or re-copy it rather than reuse it."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/db.py",
    "line": 3923,
    "summary": "_ADVERSARIAL_FINDING_COLS is a hand-typed frozenset of the adversarial_findings columns a findings-block entry may set (lens, subject_table, subject_key, claim_attacked, method, artefact, verdict, severity) — a curated mirror of the table's own schema shape, introduced in the same diff that deletes _VALID_CONFLICT_STATUS/_VALID_ITEM_STATUS/_VALID_RUN_STATUS for being exactly this shape (a hard-coded copy of something the schema already states) and widens derived_not_curated_audit.py to catch it.",
    "deeper_fix": "Derive the allowed key set from PRAGMA table_info(adversarial_findings) minus the columns the writer itself stamps or that a later verb sets (pass_id, disposition, disposition_ref, disposed_by_session, disposed_at, created_by_session, created_at), the same way dbcore already derives vocabularies from CHECKs. Because this is a column-name mirror rather than a CHECK-value mirror, it is also invisible to the very Class 3/4/5 detector this diff just built for RC2 — the detector closed the value-vocabulary instance of 'curated list mirrors the schema' but the same shape re-enters the codebase one file later as a column-name list, showing the fix treated the specific shapes named in the DR rather than the general principle (rule 8) they instantiate."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/audit/adversarial_pass_audit.py",
    "line": 42,
    "summary": "REPO_ROOT and DB_PATH are recomputed locally (Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2], and manual GUIDEBOOK_DB_PATH-or-default logic) instead of using the already-general dbcore.REPO_ROOT / dbcore.db_path(), even though this script is a sibling of two other new audit scripts in the same diff that also each redefine their own REPO_ROOT rather than importing it.",
    "deeper_fix": "Import dbcore (as supersession_backpointer_audit.py and research_tooling_separation.py already do for other reasons) and use dbcore.REPO_ROOT / dbcore.db_path() directly, so the repo-root and DB-path resolution has the one home rule 8 asks for. More generally, all three new audit scripts copy-paste the same REPO_ROOT/EXAMINED/VERDICT/exit-code boilerplate rather than drawing on a shared scripts/audit/_common.py; this diff was the opportunity to introduce that shared base rather than adding three more standalone instances of the pattern (consistent with ~30 pre-existing audit scripts doing the same, but this diff entrenches rather than reduces that count)."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/audit/supersession_backpointer_audit.py",
    "line": 50,
    "summary": "The script reaches directly into dbcore's underscore-prefixed 'private' regex objects (dbcore._LEDGER_SUPERSEDES_RE, dbcore._LEDGER_LINE_MARKER_RE at lines 50 and 61/88) rather than dbcore exposing a public per-line normalisation primitive, because dbcore's public surface (normalise_ledger_quote/count_ledger_quote) only offers a whole-text-joined form and this script needs a line-level variant to find paragraph boundaries.",
    "deeper_fix": "Add the missing public primitive to dbcore.py (e.g. dbcore.strip_ledger_line_marker(line) and a public SUPERSEDES_RE) so the grammar and normalisation genuinely have one home with a public interface, rather than dbcore holding the general logic privately and the one external caller that needs a finer-grained view of it reaching past the underscore boundary to get it."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/migrations/097_adversarial_passes_and_findings.sql",
    "line": 97,
    "summary": "The verdict=SUSTAINED ⇔ severity-required pairing is enforced only in Python (record_adversarial_pass, db.py line ~4114), per the migration's own comment 'the writer enforces the pairing; the CHECK only enforces the vocabulary' — even though the same migration proves a cross-column CHECK is available and used for a structurally identical invariant three lines earlier (CHECK (reviewer_transcript <> author_transcript), line 79).",
    "deeper_fix": "Express the pairing at the schema layer, e.g. CHECK ((verdict = 'SUSTAINED') = (severity IS NOT NULL)), so the invariant holds for every future writer or data migration that touches this table, not just this one Python call path — the same depth-of-enforcement the DR already chose for the self-administered-pass refusal in the same table."
  }
]
```

Context: focus files scripts/dbcore.py, migration 097, schemas/adversarial.py, governance/*.yaml, CLAUDE.md, and the derived_not_curated_audit.py Class 3-5 widening were themselves well-targeted at the right depth (e.g., normalise_ledger_quote/count_ledger_quote/schema_range genuinely live in dbcore.py and are reused correctly by both the writer and the audit script for RC3; the RC2 detector classes correctly generalize past the single argparse-choices= shape). The findings above are the places where a new mechanism repeats, inside this same diff, the exact "special case instead of shared/general fix" shape that RC2 and the DR's rule-8 framing were built to eliminate elsewhere.

